["owl:NamedIndividual","owl:Class"],"d3f:attack-id":"T1134.004","d3f:definition":"Adversaries may spoof the parent process identifier (PPID) of a new process to evade process-monitoring defenses or to elevate privileges. New processes are typically spawned directly from their parent, or calling, process unless explicitly specified. One way of explicitly assigning the PPID of a new process is via the <code>CreateProcess</code> API call, which supports a parameter that defines the PPID to use.(Citation: DidierStevens SelectMyParent Nov 2009) This functionality is used by Windows features such as User Account Control (UAC) to correctly set the PPID after a requested elevated process is spawned by SYSTEM (typically via <code>svchost.exe</code> or <code>consent.exe</code>) rather than the current user context.(Citation: Microsoft UAC Nov 2018)","d3f:invokes":{"@id":"d3f:CreateProcess"},"rdfs:label":"Parent PID Spoofing","rdfs:subClassOf":{"@id":"d3f:T1134"}}]},"subtechniques":{"@context":{"rdfs":"http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#","owl":"http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#","d3f":"http://d3fend.mitre.org/ontologies/d3fend.owl#","skos":"http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#"},"@graph":[{"@id":"d3f:T1134","d3f:attack-id":["T1134"],"d3f:top-level":["true"],"rdfs:hasSubClass":[{"@id":"d3f:T1134.001"},{"@id":"d3f:T1134.005"},{"@id":"d3f:T1134.004"},{"@id":"d3f:T1134.002"},{"@id":"d3f:T1134.003"}],"rdfs:label":["Access Token Manipulation"]},{"@id":"d3f:T1134.001","d3f:attack-id":["T1134.001"],"rdfs:label":["Token Impersonation/Theft"],"rdfs:subClassOf":[{"@id":"d3f:T1134"}]},{"@id":"d3f:T1134.002","d3f:attack-id":["T1134.002"],"rdfs:label":["Create Process with Token"],"rdfs:subClassOf":[{"@id":"d3f:T1134"}]},{"@id":"d3f:T1134.003","d3f:attack-id":["T1134.003"],"rdfs:label":["Make and Impersonate Token"],"rdfs:subClassOf":[{"@id":"d3f:T1134"}]},{"@id":"d3f:T1134.004","d3f:attack-id":["T1134.004"],"rdfs:label":["Parent PID Spoofing"],"rdfs:subClassOf":[{"@id":"d3f:T1134"}]},{"@id